@startuml

salt
{
  <b>ORE STUDIO - REPORTING: CREATE A REPORT</b>
  --
  New definition | Party ACMCOR | Tenant administrator
  --
  <b>THE RAIL: COMPUTED FROM WHAT THE REPORT RUNS</b>
  {#
    Step | Entry | State
    Y  | What it is | done
    Y  | The conventions it binds | done
    Y  | The pricing engines it binds | done
    Y  | The market data it binds | done
    Y  | The curve configuration it binds | done
    Y  | The run settings it binds | done
    7  | What it runs: the switchboard | current
    | <b>the analytic steps: one per analytic that is on, in display_order</b> |
    8  | Net present value | ahead
    9  | Simulation | ahead
    10 | Potential future exposure | ahead
    | <b>the final step</b> |
    11 | Review and save | ahead, final
  }
  Seven fixed steps, then one step for every analytic that is on, then the final
  review. The rail is one list, so the rail, the step numbering and the step foot
  agree at every moment: turning an analytic on adds its entry and renumbers the
  ones after it, and turning one off removes it and renumbers them again.
  --
  <b>THE RAIL AT LENGTH</b>
  --
  Fifteen or more entries is a normal length, so the column sticks to the top of
  the page and scrolls inside itself, the entries are denser, an analytic entry
  carries its code, a divider opens the analytic run and another opens the
  review, and a caption states the shape: "11 steps: 7 fixed, 3 analytics in
  display order, and the final review". The current entry is scrolled into view
  after every render. The grammar does not change: the same two-column grid, the
  same done, current and ahead marks, the same step foot.
  --
  <b>DEEP LINKS</b>
  --
  {#
    Link | Resolves to
    ?step=7 | position 7 of the current rail
    ?step=9&analytic=simulation | the same entry, named as well as numbered
    ?analytic=pfe | that analytic's step, whatever its number
  }
  The number moves when an analytic before it is turned on or off; the code does
  not. Navigation writes both into the URL, so a reload lands on the same step.
  An analytic the configuration does not carry resolves to What it runs with its
  row marked and the reason stated, never to a step that does not exist.
  --
  <b>STEP 1 - WHAT IT IS</b>
  --
  {#
    Field | Value
    Name | Intraday Exposure
    Description | Exposure profiles
    Report type | risk
    Is official | yes
    Concurrency policy | skip
  }
  The report type is a combo box over ores_reporting_report_types_tbl. risk is
  the one type registered today and it names report_execution_workflow. More
  types are expected, so the control reads the table rather than a fixed pair.
  The party comes from the session and is not a choice.
  --
  <b>STEPS 2 TO 6 - ONE STEP FOR EACH CONFIGURATION TYPE</b>
  --
  Five steps, in the order report_type_configuration_types states and the seeder
  writes: conventions, pricing_engines, todays_market, curve_configuration,
  risk_report_config. Every one carries the same four parts, so two steps can be
  read side by side: what binding that configuration means, where its data
  lives, the picker, and what the binding reaches and what it does not.
  --
  <b>STEP 2 - THE CONVENTIONS IT BINDS</b>
  --
  {#
    Part | What the step says
    Means | The market terms every trade in the run is read with
    Data | ores.refdata.bond_yield_convention.org, and 24 more
    Screen | PATHS.conventions = /refdata/conventions
    Reaches | Every instrument family, its day count and its calendars
    Not | It creates no convention and names no market data
  }
  {
    [ Group conventions ]
  }
  Binding it decides what a trade means. The same trade read under two convention
  sets gives two different numbers, and neither of them is a mistake.
  --
  <b>STEP 3 - THE PRICING ENGINES IT BINDS</b>
  --
  {#
    Part | What the step says
    Means | The model and the engine each product is priced with
    Data | pricing_model_config, pricing_model_product, its parameters
    Screen | no screen holds a pricing engine configuration
    Reaches | Every analytic that prices a trade, npv first
    Not | It names no market data and builds no curve
  }
  {
    [ Group pricing engines ]
  }
  Binding it decides how a trade is priced. A product the configuration carries no
  row for has no model in this run.
  --
  <b>STEP 4 - THE MARKET DATA IT BINDS</b>
  --
  {#
    Part | What the step says
    Means | The data a run prices against, by market role
    Data | todays_market_collection, configuration and its bindings
    Screen | no screen holds a today's market configuration
    Reaches | Every price, and the quotes each curve is built from
    Not | It holds references by name, and fixes no valuation date
  }
  {
    [ Group today's market ]
  }
  Binding it decides which configuration block each market role reads, and the
  role binding is written with configuration_market_bindings.put.
  --
  <b>STEP 5 - THE CURVE CONFIGURATION IT BINDS</b>
  --
  {#
    Part | What the step says
    Means | The recipes a run builds its curves from
    Data | curve_configuration and curve_configuration_section
    Screen | no screen holds a curve configuration
    Reaches | Every analytic that discounts, projects or reads a volatility
    Not | It holds no quote, and it builds nothing
  }
  {
    [ Group curve configuration ]
  }
  Binding it decides which recipes the run has. Without the recipe the curve is
  not built, whatever the market quotes.
  --
  <b>STEP 6 - THE RUN SETTINGS IT BINDS</b>
  --
  {#
    Part | What the step says
    Means | Base currency, observation model, threading, VaR and SIMM settings
    Data | risk_report_config, and its portfolio and book junction tables
    Screen | no screen holds a risk report configuration
    Reaches | Run behaviour, the analytics, and the portfolio and book scope
    Not | It carries no market data, no curve and no model
  }
  {
    [ Exposure simulation - Group Treasury Portfolio ]
  }
  The scope lives here and not on the definition, which is why the definition
  chooses no portfolio. No operation in this journey's set writes the settings or
  the scope: both come from the seeder.
  --
  <b>STEP 7 - WHAT IT RUNS: THE SWITCHBOARD, AND THE INDEX OF THE STEPS AFTER IT</b>
  --
  {#
    On | Analytic | Order | Step | One-line summary
    Y | npv | 1 | 8 | one present value per trade, netted to book
    Y | simulation | 2 | 9 | simulated paths, and the cube pfe reads
    Y | pfe | 3 | 10 | an exposure profile per counterparty and date
    N | saccr | 4 | -- | the row is kept at display_order 4
  }
  Fourteen analytic types, one row each: the switch, the display order and a
  one-line summary of what the analytic produces. This step is the index, not a
  place to edit settings. A row that is on states the number of the step it owns
  and carries a control that goes straight to it.
  --
  <b>TURNING ONE ON, AND TURNING ONE OFF</b>
  --
  The switch is how an analytic is added. Turning a type on writes its
  configuration_analytic row with the default value of every parameter and gives
  it a step in display_order. Turning one off asks first, because the settings
  leave the rail with the step, and because the write is active false and not a
  deletion: the row keeps its display_order and its parameter rows, so a later
  reader can see the report used to produce it. Nothing on the page deletes a
  row.
  {
    [ Turn simulation off?  Its 3 parameter values go with the step. The row is
      set to active = false and is not deleted.  ( Turn simulation off ) ( Keep it on ) ]
  }
  --
  <b>ANALYTIC STEP 8 - NET PRESENT VALUE, ONE OF THREE SUCH STEPS</b>
  --
  What net present value is: what the book is worth today, priced against today's
  curves and added up the book tree.
  How it is calculated: each trade's future cashflows are projected, discounted
  at the discount curve and summed, and the sums roll up from trade to book to
  portfolio.
  {
    Parameter | Control | Note
    discount_curve | text | the curve the cashflows are discounted at
    include_cashflows | switch | write each cashflow row as well as the total
  }
  Every parameter carries a control chosen for the shape of its value, a note on
  what it means and what a normal value looks like. An analytic that takes no
  parameter says so rather than showing an empty list.
  <b>The settings below are rows of Exposure simulation - Group Treasury
  Portfolio, not of this definition:</b> 6 definitions bind that configuration,
  so a value changed here reaches their next run too.
  {
    [ Turn npv off ]  ...  ( Back ) ( Next: simulation )
  }
  --
  <b>THE pfe AND simulation PAIR IS A STATE, NOT A BLOCKED CLICK</b>
  --
  Turning pfe on is allowed: its step appears, and the rule strip on step 7 marks
  both the pfe row and the simulation row. The pfe step states that it cannot run,
  and the save refuses the pair.
  pfe reads the cube that simulation writes. The rule lives in the ACME seeder, in
  generate_risk_config_data.py at check_exposure_needs_simulation, and nothing in
  the reporting service validates it when a definition is written. The screen
  refuses the pair itself, and a build would have to carry the rule again.
  --
  <b>FINAL STEP - REVIEW AND SAVE</b>
  --
  {#
    Written | Operation
    definition | report_definitions.put
    five binds | report_configurations.put
    analytic rows | configuration_analytics.put_many
    param rows | configuration_analytic_parameters.put
    the scope | no operation
  }
  Every field value is named, including the ones that stay empty: name Intraday
  Exposure, report_type risk, is_official true, concurrency_policy skip,
  schedule_expression none, scheduler_job_id none, fsm_state_id none,
  run_grant_id none, pre_processing execute, prepared_input_key null,
  post_processing execute. Also the analytic rows with their active and
  display_order, which are the analytic steps of the rail in that order; their
  parameter values; and the portfolio scope the bound configuration carries.
  {
    [ Save the report ]
  }
  The read-back states the stored row with report_definitions.get, that the
  definition is on demand, and that it has never run.
  This step is final, so its rail entry carries the final tag and its foot
  carries no Back. Going back is refused at the first step and at a final step.
  --
  <b>WHAT THIS SCREEN CANNOT SHOW</b>
  --
  No operation answers what a report needs in order to run beyond the
  configuration types its report type requires. Nothing validates the
  pfe-needs-simulation rule on write. No operation writes the portfolio scope or
  the run settings. Four of the five configuration types have no screen. No
  operation stages an analytic set for one definition, so the rail is a view over
  rows already written to a shared configuration. The rail primitive cannot mark
  an unfit step, group its entries or collapse them. No BFF route serves
  reporting, and nothing in the application creates a report today.
}

@enduml
